{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#2622","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#2622","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#2622\",\"parent_revision\":\"093b684750f320bc1c50d0072a97f1553d159fb9\",\"root_revision\":\"b2c6e85e8ae594441c747eeaafe07126f55c2723aeb4a11ba9204a66eb038b22\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P2\\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-12\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nThe ARL (Autonomy-Restraint Ladder) human touchpoint model defines escalation criteria: unbound constraints, domain knowledge gaps, high-risk irreversible changes, novel architecture decisions, and 3-NEEDS_WORK retry loops. These are documented in `sam-definition.md` and `CLAUDE.md`.\\n\\nVerified by G7 complexity-routing layer (2026-06-08): `sam-definition.md` and `human-touchpoint-model.md` document the escalation criteria. No conditional branch in `implement-feature` or `complete-implementation` reads constraint type, risk tier, or iteration count to trigger escalation. The ARL criteria are applied by the orchestrator's judgment, not enforced by a skill gate.\\n\\nImpact: ARL escalation is advisory, not structural. An orchestrator that does not apply ARL judgment produces no touchpoints regardless of constraint severity. The 3-NEEDS_WORK loop escalation in complete-implementation's recursive follow-up handling is the one partially-enforced case.\\n\\n## Fact-Check\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:33:59.703394Z</sub>\\n\\nVerified against primary sources (2026-06-09):\\n\\n**Claim: \\\"No conditional branch in implement-feature reads constraint type, risk tier, or iteration count\\\"**\\nVERIFIED. Read `plugins/development-harness/skills/implement-feature/SKILL.md` in full. The Progress Loop contains no mention of ARL, constraint type, risk tier, UNBOUND, or escalation. The word \\\"escalat\\\" does not appear in the file. The only gate-like behavior is the `autonomy_mode` check (`per_task` / `full_auto` / `checkpoint`) which governs dispatch pacing, not ARL escalation.\\n\\n**Claim: \\\"No conditional branch in complete-implementation reads constraint type, risk tier, or iteration count\\\"**\\nPARTIALLY VERIFIED. `complete-implementation/SKILL.md` contains `{fix_cycle}` (max 3, in the T1 blocking code review fix loop) \u2014 this IS an iteration count check. However, this is limited to the T1 code review loop. No constraint type or risk tier check exists. The S6 NEEDS_WORK loop (managed in `forensic-review/SKILL.md`) and S7 NOT_CERTIFIED loop have no counters in those skills.\\n\\n**Claim: \\\"sam-definition.md and human-touchpoint-model.md document the escalation criteria\\\"**\\nVERIFIED. `sam-definition.md` lines 119-122 state the escalation criteria explicitly including \\\"After 3 NEEDS_WORK loops on the same task, escalate to human\\\" and \\\"After 2 NOT_CERTIFIED loops in S7, escalate to human.\\\" `human-touchpoint-model.md` contains a full flowchart and dynamic escalation conditions list.\\n\\n**Claim: \\\"The 3-NEEDS_WORK loop escalation in complete-implementation's recursive follow-up handling is the one partially-enforced case\\\"**\\nVERIFIED WITH CORRECTION. The `{fix_cycle}` counter in `complete-implementation` is in the T1 code review fix loop (Recursive Follow-up Handling \u2192 Step 1), not in the S6 forensic-review NEEDS_WORK loop. The S6 forensic-review loop (`forensic-review/SKILL.md`) has no counter. So the \\\"one partially-enforced case\\\" is in `complete-implementation`, not `forensic-review`.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer using Claude Code skills**, I want to **arl human touchpoint escalation criteria are documented but not wired into any skill decision node** so that **the tooling becomes more capable and complete**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: G7 complexity-routing enforcement layer, workflow-map audit 2026-06-08\\n- **Priority**: P2\\n- **Added**: 2026-06-08\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-06-09)\\n\\n### RT-ICA Assessment\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:25:59.456630Z</sub>\\n\\n- Goal: Wire ARL human touchpoint escalation criteria into skill decision nodes so escalation is structural, not advisory\\n- Status: APPROVED-WITH-GAPS\\n- AVAILABLE:\\n  - The escalation criteria are fully documented in `human-touchpoint-model.md` with a flowchart decision tree (constraint type \u2192 risk \u2192 domain knowledge \u2192 escalate/proceed)\\n  - `default-development-flow.md` names the two pre-scheduled touchpoint gates (Gate 1 after S1, Gate 2 after S4) and the dynamic escalation conditions (NEEDS_WORK loop limit 3, NOT_CERTIFIED loop limit 2)\\n  - `sam-definition.md` documents per-agent escalation criteria with Attempt Repair / Block & Ask / Fail taxonomy for all 7 pipeline stages\\n  - The `discovery/SKILL.md` and `task-decomposition/SKILL.md` skills each contain a Human Touchpoint Gate section with a Mermaid decision flowchart checking unbound constraints, domain knowledge gaps, novel architecture, and complexity\\n  - The `complete-implementation/SKILL.md` contains a partial iteration counter (`{fix_cycle}`, max 3) for the blocking code review loop \u2014 the one case where an iteration count is explicitly read\\n  - The `forensic-review/SKILL.md` NEEDS_WORK loop is described but has no counter or escalation trigger at 3 iterations\\n- DERIVABLE:\\n  - Which skills are in scope: resolvable by searching `implements.*Human Touchpoint Gate` and `escalat` in the `plugins/development-harness/skills/` subtree \u2014 discovery, task-decomposition, forensic-review, final-verification, and implement-feature are the primary candidates\\n  - Effort range: derivable from the number of skills requiring a decision node addition and the complexity of adding a counter to a stateless skill (stateless skills cannot hold counters directly \u2014 counter storage must go in the SAM plan or task field)\\n- MISSING:\\n  - Authoritative decision on WHERE iteration counts are stored for stateless skill gates (SAM task field, plan field, or a dedicated escalation-state artifact) \u2014 this is a design decision that affects multiple skills\\n  - Whether the intent is to add programmatic branches in skill prose (Mermaid nodes that instruct the agent to check a structured field) or to add actual structured fields to the SAM task/plan model\\n  - Whether the Gate 1 (S1 \u2192 S2) and Gate 2 (S4 \u2192 S5) touchpoint evaluations in discovery and task-decomposition skills are considered \\\"wired\\\" (they already have decision flowcharts) or \\\"unwired\\\" (the flowcharts exist but the orchestrator's judgment drives the outcome, not a machine-verifiable check)\\n</div>\\n\\n### Artifact Classification\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:26:42.458501Z</sub>\\n\\n- Primary artifact type: Feature (missing guardrail \u2014 a gate that should exist structurally does not)\\n- Behavioral boundary: The escalation triggers defined in `human-touchpoint-model.md` (constraint type classification, risk tier, iteration count) are read by the orchestrator via natural-language judgment, not enforced by a conditional branch in the skill decision flow that evaluates structured state\\n- Domain-semantic evaluation target: Acceptance criteria must prove that an orchestrator following the skill instruction will deterministically pause for human input when a documented escalation condition is met \u2014 without depending on the orchestrator choosing to apply the ARL model from memory\\n</div>\\n\\n### Reproducibility\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:27:09.407723Z</sub>\\n\\n1. Load `/dh:implement-feature` and read its Progress Loop section\\n2. Observe: the loop dispatches task batches, handles health checks, bookend tasks, concerns, contract verification \u2014 no step checks constraint type or risk tier before dispatching\\n3. Load `/dh:forensic-review` and read the NEEDS_WORK Remediation Loop section\\n4. Observe: the loop instructs re-execution and re-review with no counter and no escalation trigger at 3 iterations\\n5. Load `/dh:discovery` and read the Human Touchpoint Gate section\\n6. Observe: the gate evaluates unbound constraints and domain knowledge gaps \u2014 but the evaluation is a natural-language prompt instruction, not a check against a structured `constraint_type` field from a SAM task or plan artifact\\n7. Repeat step 6 for `/dh:task-decomposition`\\n8. Observe: both gates instruct the agent to \\\"evaluate\\\" or \\\"consider\\\" escalation \u2014 no branch reads a machine-readable constraint classification stored in an artifact\\n\\nResult: All escalation decisions depend on the orchestrating agent's judgment when reading the skill. A different orchestrator, a lower-capability model, or an orchestrator under context pressure may not apply the ARL logic, producing no touchpoints regardless of constraint severity.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Output / Evidence\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:27:36.225311Z</sub>\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/implement-feature/SKILL.md` \u2014 full Progress Loop section has no mention of ARL, constraint type, risk tier, UNBOUND, or escalation\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/complete-implementation/SKILL.md` \u2014 contains `{fix_cycle}` counter (max 3) for T1 blocking code review loop only; no equivalent for S6 NEEDS_WORK or S7 NOT_CERTIFIED loops\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/forensic-review/SKILL.md` \u2014 NEEDS_WORK Remediation Loop has no iteration counter and no escalation branch at 3 iterations\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/development-harness/references/default-development-flow.md` \u2014 documents \\\"Loop limit: 3 NEEDS_WORK iterations per task before human escalation\\\" and \\\"Loop limit: 2 NOT_CERTIFIED iterations before human escalation\\\" as prose descriptions only; no skill reads these limits from a stored counter\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/development-harness/references/human-touchpoint-model.md` \u2014 complete escalation flowchart with constraint classification, risk assessment, and domain knowledge check \u2014 never referenced by `implement-feature` or `complete-implementation`\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/discovery/SKILL.md` and `plugins/development-harness/skills/task-decomposition/SKILL.md` \u2014 both contain Human Touchpoint Gate flowcharts that check unbound constraints, domain knowledge gaps, novel architecture \u2014 these ARE decision nodes, but they evaluate by agent judgment against natural-language descriptions, not against stored structured fields\\n</div>\\n\\n### Priority\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:27:59.544624Z</sub>\\n\\n6/10 \u2014 The ARL criteria are documented and partially present (discovery and task-decomposition skills have Human Touchpoint Gate flowcharts). The gap is primarily in: (1) the NEEDS_WORK/NOT_CERTIFIED iteration loop escalation in forensic-review and final-verification, where the documented 3-iteration and 2-iteration limits have no enforcement mechanism, and (2) the implement-feature dispatch loop having no pre-dispatch constraint classification check. The risk is that under context pressure or with a lower-capability model, loop limits are silently exceeded and irreversible actions proceed without human review. This is a correctness gap in the guardrail layer, not a missing feature. Rated P2 because the existing natural-language instructions provide partial coverage; the gap manifests as unreliability rather than complete absence.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:28:19.556249Z</sub>\\n\\n- Blocks: Reliable autonomous operation at scale \u2014 high-risk or irreversible tasks can proceed through the pipeline without triggering a human touchpoint if the orchestrator does not independently apply the ARL model\\n- Bottleneck: Trust in the HOOTL (human-out-of-the-loop) mode of `implement-feature` \u2014 operators cannot be confident the pipeline will self-escalate when it should\\n- Also affects: The `complete-implementation` T1 fix loop is partially protected (max 3 cycles) but the S6 forensic review loop has no equivalent structural limit; thrashing in forensic review can loop indefinitely or stop only when the orchestrator notices\\n</div>\\n\\n### Benefits\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:28:49.010078Z</sub>\\n\\n- Operators can trust the pipeline to self-escalate on high-risk or ambiguous tasks without monitoring every run\\n- The NEEDS_WORK loop limit becomes a structural guarantee rather than a best-effort instruction, preventing agent thrashing on intractable tasks\\n- ARL constraint classification becomes a machine-readable artifact that downstream stages can query, supporting future automation of escalation routing\\n- Brings the implementation into alignment with the documented SAM specification in `sam-definition.md` and `human-touchpoint-model.md`\\n</div>\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:29:17.820316Z</sub>\\n\\nWhen a skill decision node reaches a condition that matches the ARL escalation criteria, the skill's Mermaid decision flowchart contains a branch that evaluates a machine-readable signal (stored field, loop counter, structured classification) and routes to a human escalation output rather than relying on the agent's judgment.\\n\\nSpecifically:\\n\\n**Gate 1 (S1 \u2192 S2, discovery skill):** The Human Touchpoint Gate already has a decision flowchart. The improvement would ensure the constraint classification produced by the discovery agent is stored in a structured field of the discovery artifact so the gate reads a field, not the agent's memory.\\n\\n**Gate 2 (S4 \u2192 S5, task-decomposition skill):** Same pattern \u2014 the complexity and novelty assessment is stored in a structured field that the gate's decision node reads explicitly.\\n\\n**S6 NEEDS_WORK loop (forensic-review skill):** A loop counter is stored in the SAM task field (e.g., `needs_work_count`). The forensic-review skill's orchestration checks the counter before dispatching another review cycle: when `needs_work_count >= 3`, the skill outputs a Gastown-format escalation payload and stops the loop.\\n\\n**S7 NOT_CERTIFIED loop (final-verification skill):** Same pattern with `not_certified_count >= 2`.\\n\\n**implement-feature (pre-dispatch):** Before dispatching a task batch, the implement-feature skill checks the plan's constraint classification field (if populated by discovery or task-decomposition) and routes to a human touchpoint when the classification indicates unbound constraints or high-risk irreversible changes.\\n\\nThe escalation output in all cases follows the Gastown format defined in `sam-definition.md`: Stage, Context (bound constraints), Blocker (unbound constraints or loop limit), Decision Options.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Desired Structure\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:29:57.274901Z</sub>\\n\\nThe target state after this work:\\n\\n1. **`forensic-review/SKILL.md`** contains a NEEDS_WORK loop counter check: the orchestration step before re-dispatching reads `needs_work_count` from the SAM task (via `sam_task(action='read')`) and compares it to the limit (3). When the limit is reached, the skill outputs a Gastown-format escalation message and stops.\\n\\n2. **`final-verification/SKILL.md`** contains a NOT_CERTIFIED loop counter check with the same pattern (limit: 2).\\n\\n3. **`sam-definition.md`** and `human-touchpoint-model.md` are updated to reference the structured field name used for the counter, so the documentation matches the implementation.\\n\\n4. **`default-development-flow.md`** loop-limit prose entries point to the skill sections that enforce them.\\n\\n5. **`discovery/SKILL.md`** and **`task-decomposition/SKILL.md`** Human Touchpoint Gate sections are reviewed: if their existing decision flowcharts already provide sufficient structural guidance (even if judgment-based), they may be out of scope or require only minor additions to store the constraint classification.\\n\\n6. **`implement-feature/SKILL.md`** is reviewed for whether a pre-dispatch constraint check is warranted or whether Gate 1 and Gate 2 are sufficient upstream guards.\\n\\nThe SAM task model (`sam_schema/core/models.py`) may need a new field such as `needs_work_count: int` and `not_certified_count: int` \u2014 this is a planning decision for the implementer.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:30:29.289007Z</sub>\\n\\n1. Given a task that has received 3 consecutive NEEDS_WORK verdicts from `@dh:code-reviewer`, the forensic-review skill stops the loop and outputs a Gastown-format escalation message (Stage, Context, Blocker, Decision Options) instead of dispatching a fourth review cycle. The output is observable in the orchestrator's terminal or session log.\\n\\n2. Given a task plan that has received 2 consecutive NOT_CERTIFIED verdicts from `@dh:final-verification`, the final-verification skill stops the loop and outputs a Gastown-format escalation message. The output is observable in the orchestrator's terminal or session log.\\n\\n3. Given a task whose `needs_work_count` field is 2 (before the third cycle), the forensic-review skill dispatches the review normally without escalation.\\n\\n4. Given a discovery stage that identifies unbound constraints and stores them in the discovery artifact as a structured field, the `discovery/SKILL.md` Human Touchpoint Gate reads that field to determine whether to escalate \u2014 the decision is not solely based on the agent re-reading the natural-language discovery document.\\n\\n5. The `default-development-flow.md` loop-limit statements (\\\"3 NEEDS_WORK iterations per task\\\" and \\\"2 NOT_CERTIFIED iterations before human escalation\\\") have corresponding cross-references to the skill sections that enforce them, so the documentation and implementation do not silently diverge.\\n\\n6. Given a fresh run of `/dh:implement-feature` against a plan with no prior NEEDS_WORK history, the loop counter field starts at 0 and no escalation fires.\\n\\n7. The escalation output in all cases matches the Gastown format defined in `sam-definition.md`: the message includes Stage, Trigger, Bound Constraints (what the agent knows), Unbound Constraints or Loop Limit (what triggered escalation), and Decision Options (concrete choices to unblock).\\n</div>\\n\\n### Questions for Human\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:31:00.097194Z</sub>\\n\\n- Where should the NEEDS_WORK and NOT_CERTIFIED iteration counters live? Options: (a) a new field on the SAM `Task` model (`needs_work_count: int`), (b) a new field on the SAM `Plan` model keyed by task ID, (c) stored in a dedicated escalation-state artifact registered via `artifact_register`. Option (a) requires a schema change to `sam_schema/core/models.py` and migration handling.\\n\\n- For the Gate 1 and Gate 2 touchpoints in `discovery/SKILL.md` and `task-decomposition/SKILL.md`: these already have Mermaid decision flowcharts that instruct escalation on unbound constraints and novel architecture. Are these considered \\\"wired\\\" for the purposes of this item, or does \\\"wired\\\" specifically require that the evaluation reads a structured field value rather than relying on the agent's judgment from the natural-language discovery output?\\n\\n- Is the pre-dispatch constraint check in `implement-feature` in scope? The item description says \\\"no conditional branch in implement-feature reads constraint type, risk tier, or iteration count\\\" \u2014 but given that Gate 1 and Gate 2 are upstream guards, is adding a check to implement-feature redundant or necessary defense-in-depth?\\n</div>\\n\\n### Resources\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:31:32.387373Z</sub>\\n\\n| Type | Item |\\n|------|------|\\n| Reference | `plugins/development-harness/skills/development-harness/references/human-touchpoint-model.md` \u2014 authoritative ARL escalation flowchart and constraint types |\\n| Reference | `plugins/development-harness/skills/development-harness/references/default-development-flow.md` \u2014 loop limits documented (S6: 3, S7: 2) |\\n| Reference | `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/work/sam-definition.md` \u2014 per-agent escalation criteria taxonomy |\\n| Skill | `/dh:forensic-review` \u2014 `plugins/development-harness/skills/forensic-review/SKILL.md` \u2014 NEEDS_WORK loop has no counter |\\n| Skill | `/dh:final-verification` \u2014 `plugins/development-harness/skills/final-verification/SKILL.md` \u2014 NOT_CERTIFIED loop (verify counter state) |\\n| Skill | `/dh:implement-feature` \u2014 `plugins/development-harness/skills/implement-feature/SKILL.md` \u2014 no ARL check in Progress Loop |\\n| Skill | `/dh:discovery` \u2014 `plugins/development-harness/skills/discovery/SKILL.md` \u2014 has Human Touchpoint Gate with partial coverage |\\n| Skill | `/dh:task-decomposition` \u2014 `plugins/development-harness/skills/task-decomposition/SKILL.md` \u2014 has Human Touchpoint Gate with partial coverage |\\n| Skill | `/dh:complete-implementation` \u2014 `plugins/development-harness/skills/complete-implementation/SKILL.md` \u2014 partially enforced: T1 fix loop has `{fix_cycle}` counter, max 3 |\\n| Domain model | `plugins/development-harness/sam_schema/core/models.py` \u2014 authoritative Task and Plan field definitions; any new counter field must be added here |\\n| Skill | `/plugin-creator:arl` \u2014 `plugins/plugin-creator/skills/arl/SKILL.md` \u2014 ARL knowledge reference (R1-R10 gates, HOOTL design goals) |\\n</div>\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:32:03.565156Z</sub>\\n\\n- Depends on: None known \u2014 all source documents and skill files are present and readable\\n- Blocks: Reliable HOOTL (human-out-of-the-loop) execution confidence; any item that depends on the ARL enforcement layer being structural rather than advisory\\n- Related: If the loop counter requires a new Task model field, this item depends on (or creates) a schema migration task for `sam_schema/core/models.py`\\n</div>\\n\\n### Blockers\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:32:36.182721Z</sub>\\n\\n- Design decision on counter storage location (Task model field vs. Plan field vs. artifact) is unresolved \u2014 this affects which files change and whether a schema migration is required. See Questions for Human above.\\n- Scope boundary for \\\"wired\\\" is ambiguous: discovery and task-decomposition skills already have escalation decision flowcharts. If these count as \\\"wired,\\\" the primary remaining work is forensic-review and final-verification loop counters only. If they do not count, the scope expands to include adding structured constraint classification fields to the discovery artifact and updating those gates to read them.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Effort\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:33:06.352830Z</sub>\\n\\nMedium \u2014 The minimum-scope work (adding NEEDS_WORK counter to forensic-review and NOT_CERTIFIED counter to final-verification) is well-understood: add a loop counter check to two skill files. If counter storage requires a new Task model field, add a schema change to `sam_schema/core/models.py` plus migration. The broader scope (structured constraint classification in discovery artifact, implement-feature pre-dispatch check) adds two more skills and a new artifact field, which raises effort to the higher end of Medium. The 3 open design questions must be resolved before sizing the implementation plan.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Design Intent Alignment\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:34:23.043819Z</sub>\\n\\nThe development-harness plugin's stated design principle: \\\"Human escalation follows ARL constraint analysis, not arbitrary checkpoints.\\\" (Source: `plugins/development-harness/CLAUDE.md`.)\\n\\nThis item directly serves that principle. Currently, escalation follows ARL constraint analysis only when the orchestrator independently applies the `human-touchpoint-model.md` logic \u2014 which is not guaranteed by any skill decision node. Wiring the escalation criteria into skill decision nodes converts the design principle from a behavioral aspiration into a structural guarantee.\\n\\nThe `complete-implementation` T1 fix loop's `{fix_cycle}` counter demonstrates that the harness already applies this pattern in one case. This item extends the same pattern to the remaining escalation criteria, producing a consistent enforcement layer across all documented escalation conditions.\\n\\nAlignment verdict: DIRECTLY ALIGNED \u2014 this work closes the gap between the documented design intent and the skill implementation.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Grooming Complete\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:35:25.401733Z</sub>\\n\\nAll sections written. Item ready for planning phase. Open design decisions (counter storage location, scope of \\\"wired\\\", implement-feature pre-dispatch check) must be resolved before a SAM plan is created.\\n</div>\\n\\n### Impact Radius\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:34:56.225666Z</sub>\\n\\nFiles that must change to wire escalation criteria:\\n\\n**Core skill changes (minimum scope):**\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/forensic-review/SKILL.md` \u2014 add NEEDS_WORK loop counter check (escalate at 3)\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/final-verification/SKILL.md` \u2014 add NOT_CERTIFIED loop counter check (escalate at 2)\\n\\n**Schema change (if counter stored in Task model):**\\n- `plugins/development-harness/sam_schema/core/models.py` \u2014 add `needs_work_count: int` and `not_certified_count: int` fields to `Task` model\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/TASK_FILE_FORMAT.md` \u2014 add field documentation\\n\\n**Documentation consistency (required with any scope):**\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/development-harness/references/default-development-flow.md` \u2014 update loop-limit statements to reference the enforcement mechanism\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/work/sam-definition.md` \u2014 update escalation criteria section to reflect wired vs. unwired status\\n\\n**Extended scope (if discovery/task-decomposition gates are also in scope):**\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/discovery/SKILL.md` \u2014 review Human Touchpoint Gate section; optionally add structured constraint classification field write\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/task-decomposition/SKILL.md` \u2014 same review\\n\\n**Not in scope:**\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/implement-feature/SKILL.md` \u2014 the pre-dispatch constraint check is a design decision deferred to Q&A above\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/complete-implementation/SKILL.md` \u2014 T1 fix loop already has `{fix_cycle}` counter; no change needed for that loop\\n</div>\\n\\n### Issue Classification\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-06-09T08:33:29.421485Z</sub>\\n\\nmissing-guardrail \u2014 The ARL escalation model is fully documented and partially present (discovery and task-decomposition gates exist as natural-language flowcharts), but the structural enforcement layer is absent: the NEEDS_WORK and NOT_CERTIFIED iteration loop limits documented in the reference files have no corresponding counter or branch in the executing skills (`forensic-review`, `final-verification`). The one wired case \u2014 the `{fix_cycle}` counter in `complete-implementation`'s T1 fix loop \u2014 demonstrates the target pattern. This item extends that pattern to the remaining escalation criteria.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"5d6f828546d7b86903a5fed458ce5f1e5b1d15eb179460f187b95c6f1bd381a7\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABPnM3GA\"}"}